joining us is rachel mclean she's  minister for safeguarding and and she is  basically a home office  minister um and if you look just down  that lens you can ask her a question  right so my question to you minister is  my energy bills have risen by 80 pounds  a month  and  where used to be 120 pounds is now  200 pounds if not more  um if you include the petrol and things  so i'm just wanting to ask what are you  going to be doing to help people like me  families like me minister  yeah hello it's lovely to speak to you  and we we understand and we know the  impact that rising energy bills and  bills and costs of food are having on  families like yours all over the country  so  we very much do need to make sure that  the support is going to those families  the support packages that we've already  put in place  and we've already put taken action to  help people with energy bills and  there's more help coming  and i think the other point that i think  is really important that families can  i can  take account of is that we have put  money into those local communities who  often know  better than we do in central government  where the need is so  local authorities local areas have got a  household support fund that is available  for families who've got individual  circumstances they might be struggling  and i would definitely recommend any  family who is feeling the pinch at this  time to approach their their local  authority and see what help is on offer  do you want to answer your follow-up um  yeah with what you've just mentioned now  with helpful families that's available  with um within the local council  i also happen to work with with families  from  um  communities experiencing um inequalities  and my question is  if those services are available we are  finding as an organization  that  are  the people we work alongside are not  able to access those  the support that's available because of  the certain boundaries if there's a  requirement of a certain paperwork or if  there's um  forms that needs to be online and they  don't have access to that it's become a  lot more complicated and therefore  people are not um accessing that so my  question is what are you putting in  place for those people that it's so easy  to access as opposed to being so  complicated good question minister i  think that's it's a really good point  and we do recognize that not everybody  has access to the internet and of course  we want to get those funds uh targeted  to where they really need to go as  quickly as possible without excessive  paperwork i mean there has to be some  basic paperwork of course you're talking  about public money and obviously the  government would be criticized if we  didn't take those proportionate steps i  mean i would recommend in your  individual case speak to your local mp i  have this dialogue with my local  organizations often and we are often  able to sign posts and unblock specific  issues that you're having  thank you very much indeed thanks a lot  a minister i'm gonna if i could follow  up on that actually lisa nandy's the  local mp and we will be chatting to her  here in wigan during the course of the  week some of your colleagues think we  should have a government minister for  the cost of living given that inflation  is likely to hit something like 10  percent at least 10 percent we're told  by summit this year do you think that  that is a good idea  so i've not heard that specific request  but what i would say of course that's  the chancellor's job and it's the job of  the whole cabinet and the whole  government and every minister is looking  at particular costs that are impacting  on the areas that they are controlling  and looking to see where savings can be  made and where reductions can be made  because the cost of living is about  energy bills but it's also about all  those other things that you've mentioned  so i think what we need to focus on now  is over the long term we have got these  short-term pressures on us that we're  all aware of and that your families in  wigan are seeing but over the long term  we need to have a plan to grow the  economy and make sure that people are  able to protect themselves better uh  whether that is by taking on more hours  or moving to a better paid job and these  are these are long-term actions but  that's what we're focused on as a  government  you say uh taking on more hours i mean  some people that i've been chatting to  minister over the last i've moved  outside actually because obviously they  need to get ready and get the kids ready  for school you know some people are  working three jobs  they're working um every hour that god  sends them so and they're still going to  food banks um to get to get food because  they can't afford to feed their kids you  know so it's not as straightforward as  that is it  no and of course it's not and  i'm not suggesting that for one moment  but we have often heard in the past when  people are facing problems with their  budgets that one of the obstacles and it  may not be for everybody one of the  obstacles is about being able to take on  more hours or even move to a better paid  job and of course it's an individual  situation depending on on that  particular family situation but that's  why the job centres exist that's why the  work coaches exist that's why we put the  support into those job centres to work  with individuals on their own individual  situation so it may be right for some  people they may be able to access  additional hours but of course it's it's  not going to work for people who are  already  working in three jobs and that's why we  need to have the other measures in place  such as the um all the help that we're  putting into the schools uh the the the  help with the the the um local  authorities help and support that i've  already mentioned and these are where  we're going to target the help where  it's most needed  i'm reading in my telegraph this morning  that  your boss the prime minister is heading  to northern ireland today and he is  blaming the northern ireland protocol on  the cost of living crisis hi  so  so if you read what the prime minister  set out in in that article which i think  is a very  balanced piece  he's making it clear that he needs to go  to northern ireland because we need to  have a functioning executive and that is  how with that strong local leadership  the parties can come together  and tackle the cost of living crisis but  of course the situation is urgent  that's why the prime minister is going  to northern ireland to get around the  table with all those parties to find a  way through this  but explain to me how the northern  ireland protocol is damaging the cost of  living  because what's the situation with the  northern ireland protocol is putting  additional costs and paperwork on  businesses  it's affecting the supply of various  products and of course that has a  knock-on impact on the cost of living  and all of the parties in the recent  elections  have called for changes to be made for  the protocol everyone acknowledges that  it's not working so that's why it is  really important that we see hopefully  we see some movement from the eu but the  prime minister has been clear that  nothing is off the table the situation  needs to be resolved for the benefit of  the peace and security of the people of  northern ireland  i thought it was an oven-ready deal  theresa may we didn't like her deal so  we got a new prime minister boris  johnson told us it was an oven ready  deal and it's an international treaty  i think you're talking about two  different things there we have a trade  and cooperation agreement with our  european partners that was agreed after  the northern ireland protocol was signed  and inherently built into the protocol  is a consent mechanism and requirements  for all parties to work in good faith to  protect those overriding important  principles of the belfast good friday  agreement now clearly there are issues  with how the protocol is working don't  forget it was also signed before kovid  it was signed before a war in ukraine  it is incumbent on all parties to come  together now and and work in good faith  to resolve these practical issues and i  think you know if you look at the  dialogue we've had with the eu we've  been working really hard to put forward  sensible and proportionate proposals to  our eu partners  and we do need to see movement from them  on a number of areas i'll just point to  one uh one particular issue which is  we've we've made proposals and we've set  up um a  an i.t system to ease trade flows and  unfortunately the eu haven't even  implemented that they've not even  started to use it so we've taken a lot  of steps on our side and we really do  want to see that movement now from our  european friends  just to clarify the prime minister is  blaming the situation in ukraine for the  border down the irish sea  no that's not what i said at all i  pointed to a range of factors that have  taken place before  since the protocol was signed and since  the trade and corporation agreement was  negotiated all of which have a bearing  and all parties that are part of this  have a responsibility to the peace and  security of northern ireland  uh but i suppose the um european  government would say we looked at this  we realized it was a bit complicated as  far as the paperwork is concerned and  they say that they've scrapped something  like 80  of the paperwork that was previously  necessary as part of the northern  ireland uh protocol so um who's right is  it british government right or is the  european union right and if we do um  decide that we're going to scrap parts  of the northern ireland protocol are you  content that it would be within the law  and you would vote for it  so i think on your first point of course  there are always going to be measures  that have to be negotiated and i think  that's the whole that's the point of a  negotiation with our european partners  this has been going on at pace with  officials with our with our  interlocutors uh on the european side  and as far as the uh subsequent actions  that are going to be taken  a lot of it is speculation but of course  the uk is a country that always lives up  to its international legal obligations  and we will continue to do that  okay just before i let you go minister i  know that you're very busy this morning  we appreciate you taking the time and i  know that mickey appreciates you  answering her questions um i can see  that there are some suggestions that uh  civil servant numbers should be uh cut  in and around uh white hole some 200  miles south of where we are now perhaps  something like 20 in the home office  will the home office still uh work  effectively with a 20 cut in staff i  think it's always right to look at where  taxpayers money is being spent including  on the civil service and i know from my  time in government that we have added  more people rightly so to help us get  through not only the kovid pandemic but  also those brexit negotiations it's  always right to keep those numbers under  review what we're seeing  in a number of government departments is  actually moving them out of london so  they are closer to the communities such  as wicked and i think that's a really  positive step  okay it's good to talk to you minister  thanks very much indeed for joining us  thanks a lot

